From 804e4f5c3aac4ad676bfd2cba52db6fcbea410b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Wakely <jwakely@redhat.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 12:32:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] libstdc++: Fix debug mode for pb_ds ordered-vector trees
 [PR127675]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

As noted in the bugzilla PR, there are debug mode assertions in the
ov_tree_map and ov_tree_set code which fail occasionally (but not
consistently because the tests use the current time as a random seed).

This change first allows the time-based random seed to be overridden
using the same GLIBCXX_SEED_TEST_RNG environment variable already
checked elsewhere in the testsuite. This allows failures to be triggered
reliably.

The bug in erase_if is an exception-safety bug where most state is
rolled back cleanly if an exception occurs, but some modifications to
the debug_base::m_keys list are not rolled back. This leaves the
container in an inconsistent state where the values in m_keys do not
match the contents of the container. This triggers debug assertions on
the next operation when the container checks its invariants.

The bug in erase(It) is much simpler and easier to trigger: the function
calls alloc.allocate(m_size - 1) unconditionally, even if m_size == 1.
It is unspecified what allocators return for zero-sized allocations, so
this can return a non-null pointer.  That is then inconsistent with the
debug assertions which require m_a_values == nullptr to be true when
m_size == 0. The solution here is to simply avoid allocating anything
when m_size == 1.

There's a similar latent bug in the 4-param overload of
copy_from_ordered_range, which would call allocate(0) if called with two
empty ranges. Although its only caller today already checks for empty
ranges, it would be more robust to make copy_from_ordered_range safe for
this case. That avoids a potential future bug where the debug assertions
fail again because copy_from_ordered_range allocates a non-null pointer
for m_size == 0. The change made in this commit is to return early if
len == 0, which is exactly what the 2-param overload of
copy_from_ordered_range already does.

Finally, the split and join members of the debug_base are refactored to
use std::list::splice instead of inserting and erasing elements from the
two lists. This means they perform no allocations and no copies, so
cannot throw exceptions (unless the comparison function passed to split
throws).  Although this doesn't fix any known bugs, it makes the callers
of those functions more robust and makes it easier to ensure those
callers are exception-safe.

libstdc++-v3/ChangeLog:

	PR libstdc++/127675
	* include/ext/pb_ds/detail/debug_map_base.hpp (split, join): Use
	std::list::splice to move elements without reallocating.
	* include/ext/pb_ds/detail/ov_tree_map_/constructors_destructor_fn_imps.hpp
	(copy_from_ordered_range(It, It, It, It)): Do not allocate new
	storage for an empty container.
	* include/ext/pb_ds/detail/ov_tree_map_/erase_fn_imps.hpp
	(erase_if): Do not modify this->debug_base::m_keys until all
	allocations and other potentially-throwing operations have
	completed successfully.
	(erase_imp): Do not allocate new storage for an empty container.
	* testsuite/ext/pb_ds/regression/127675.cc: New test.
	* testsuite/util/testsuite_rng.h (get_time_determined_seed): Use
	getenv("GLIBCXX_SEED_TEST_RNG") to allow the seed to be fixed.

Reviewed-by: Tomasz Kamiński <tkaminsk@redhat.com>
(cherry picked from commit c1a660d44be3dc140b7ff24611e8ff7a4557d761)
